     } else {\n340\t            self.broadcast_clients(message);\n341\t        }\n342\t    }\n343\t}\n344\t\n345\tfn try_reserve_queue_bytes(counter: &AtomicUsize, bytes: usize, max_bytes: usize) -> bool {\n\n... (gap) ...\n\n736\t    Ok(credentials)\n737\t}\n738\t\n739\tfn read_chrome_messages(state: SharedState) -> Result<()> {\n740\t    let stdin = io::stdin();\n741\t    let mut reader = stdin.lock();\n742\t    while let Some(message) =\n743\t        read_frame(&mut reader).context(\"extension-host: platform reader error\")?\n744\t    {\n745\t        handle_chrome_message(&state, message);\n746\t    }\n747\t    Ok(())\n748\t}\n749\t\n750\tfn read_client_messages(state: SharedState, client_id: usize, stream: UnixStream) {\n751\t    let mut stream = stream;\n752\t    loop {\n753\t        match read_frame(&mut stream) {\n754\t            Ok(Some(message)) => handle_client_message(&state, client_id, message),\n755\t            Ok(None) => break,\n756\t            Err(error) => {\n757\t                log(&format!(\"client socket read error: {error}\"));\n758\t                break;\n759\t            }\n760\t        }\n761\t    }\n762\t\n763\t    disconnect_client(&state, client_id);\n764\t}\n765\t\n766\tfn write_client_messages(\n767\t    state: Weak<Mutex<HostState>>,\n\n... (gap) ...\n\n786\t    state.remove_client(client_id);\n787\t}\n788\t\n789\tfn handle_client_message(state: &SharedState, client_id: usize, message: Value) {\n790\t    {\n791\t        let mut state = state.lock().expect(\"host state mutex poisoned\");\n792\t        state.prune_expired_pending_requests(Instant::now());\n793\t        if !state.clients.contains_key(&client_id) {\n794\t            return;\n795\t        }\n796\t    }\n797\t\n798\t    if is_response(&message) {\n799\t        let Some(id) = message_id_as_str(&message) else {\n800\t            return;\n801\t        };\n802\t\n\n... (gap) ...\n\n807\t        if pending.client_id != client_id {\n808\t            return;\n809\t        }\n810\t        state.pending_client_requests.remove(id);\n811\t        if let Some(group) = pending.fanout_group {\n812\t            if is_successful_heartbeat_response(&message) {\n813\t                state\n814\t                    .pending_client_requests\n815\t                    .retain(|_, sibling| sibling.fanout_group != Some(group));\n816\t                state.send_chrome(&with_id(message, pending.chrome_request_id));\n817\t            } else if !state\n818\t                .pending_client_requests\n819\t                .values()\n820\t                .any(|sibling| sibling.fanout_group == Some(group))\n821\t            {\n822\t                state.send_chrome(&json!({\n823\t                    \"jsonrpc\": \"2.0\",\n824\t                    \"id\": pending.chrome_request_id,\n825\t                    \"error\": {\n\n... (gap) \n[...truncated]"},"obs_mudluynw_ea2d2d94be9c":{"hookType":"post_tool_use","id":"obs_mudluynw_ea2d2d94be9c","origin":{"capturedAt":"2026-09-23T04:30:56.981Z","channel":"tool","detail":"Bash"},"raw":{"tool_input":{"command":"sed -n '2558,2575p' /home/chris/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md"},"tool_name":"Bash","tool_output":"- Compare a signed upstream ASAR and installed patched ASAR with `node --check` before changing features: the upstream `26.810.50856` ASAR parsed, while the installed patched ASAR failed at main bundle line 506. Isolation identified `computer-use-linux` and its `nix-staging-permissions` patch. [Task 4]\n- In `linux-features/computer-use-linux/patch.js`, a patch injected into a minified comma expression must stay an expression. Wrap cleanup as `await(async()=>{try{...}finally{...}})()` rather than inserting bare `try/finally`; retain parser regression coverage for the comma-expression fixture. Focused tests, both isolated and complete 16-feature `node --check`, and `git diff --check` passed. [Task 4]\n- The GUI updater rebuilds from frozen `/opt/codex-desktop/update-builder`; after fixing this checkout, an authorized recovery is `make install-native`, not Check for Updates. The fixed source was not installed/restarted in this rollout. [Task 4]\n- On CachyOS/Niri/Wayland, the bundled Computer Use backend is the correct path; doctor checks AT-SPI, Niri window control, portal input/screenshots, `/dev/uinput`, and development input. Backend/plugin registration is separate from UI visibility: use `CODEX_LINUX_ENABLE_COMPUTER_USE_UI=1` for a one-build UI opt-in. [Task 1]\n- `enabled-feature-drift` is a descriptor/config mismatch, not a bypass condition. The 27-feature attempt became accepted-with-warnings/zero blockers only after removing the eight drifted ids and rerunning direct `scripts/validate-upstream-dmg.js --enforce`; 19 features remained enabled. [Task 1]\n- After `make install-native`, compare running app/webview/plugin start times to package install time, inspect stale hashed-asset errors, fully terminate old processes, then restart. `Failed to fetch dynamically imported module` here came from an old Electron renderer after `/opt/codex-desktop` replacement, not missing Computer Use payload; post-restart cache/install SHA and doctor matched. [Task 1]\n- `linux-features/shared-app-server-socket/patch.js` must retain upstream `getConfigOverrides` and spawn the shared `app-server` authority with each generated value as repeated `-c <override>` pairs. A bundled-Codex check accepted `codex_app` as `transport: stdio` and completed `thread/start`; focused socket regression coverage passed. [Task 5]\n- Upstream `main` was fast-forwarded from `6bd3816` to `b95f2f5` while tracked local work was retained in `stash@{0}: On main: codex-pull-preserve-20260827`. Conflict intent was upstream’s current Computer Use descriptor architecture plus the local shared-socket override regression and upstream Unix-socket path helper. [Task 5]\n- Thorium has two independent surfaces: `scripts/extension-ids.json` controls discovery, while `scripts/installManifest.mjs` embeds native-messaging manifest directories. `patchManifestInstaller()` in `linux-features/thorium-chrome-plugin/patch-chrome-plugin.js` idempotently adds `.config/thorium/NativeMessagingHosts` after the Google Chrome-for-Testing anchor. [Task 6]\n- A stale Thorium manifest can yield `No compatible Codex app-server entry was found` even with registry support, because host identity is checked against current `extensionHostPath`. The observed legacy host was cache release `26.803.81509`; current runtime was `26.820.71523`. Tests passed: Thorium feature 6/6, framework 1/1, syntax checks, and `git diff --check`. [Task 6]\n- For signed package `26.820.71523`, stale feature patches fail critical drift checks: use current `origin/main` (`b95f2f5`) in a detached worktree and run `node --test scripts/lib/linux-features.test.js linux-features/*/test.js`. The validated native package had 31 user-facing features, 69 applied patches, 762 pass/0 fail, and `pacman -Qkk` found 0 altered files. `directory-only-working-tree-watch` conflicts with `shallow-repository-watches`; omit internal Nix-only `nix-store-bundled-marketplace-permissions` from native packages. [Task 7]\n- `launcher/start.sh.template` must export `CODEX_HOME`, `CODEX_LINUX_APP_DIR`, `CODEX_LINUX_APP_STATE_DIR`, `CODEX_LINUX_FEATURES_DIR`, and `CODEX_LINUX_LAUNCHER_LOG`; prelaunch/cold-start hooks receive app/state/cache-log paths, and after-exit additionally receives Electron exit status. Prove the contract with `launcher provides the documented runtime hook contract`, `node --test launcher/start.test.js` (8/8), and `bash tests/scripts_smoke.sh`. [Task 7]\n- Verify installed state through `/opt/codex-desktop/resources/codex-linux-build-info.json`, patch report, `/opt/codex-desktop/start.sh --diagnose`, and Computer Use doctor. Read Aloud can use native `spd-say`/`espeak-ng` when Kokoro assets are absent. [Task 7]\n- Current upstream splits Thorium inventory between `browserDiagnostics` and `browserExtensions`; register Thorium in both. The shared Chromium registry now lives in `.vite/build/src-*.js`, so the feature needs an `extracted-app:pre-webview` descriptor. Prelaunch/cold-start hooks regenerate the manifest from the current cache; a stale host path can fail even when discovery is correct. [Task 8]\n- On an isolated detached worktree at `origin/main` `241435e`, Thorium focused tests were 9/9, and `make pacman` produced `/tmp/codex-desktop-linux-repair-ready/dist/codex-desktop-2026.08.30.155556-1-x86_64.pkg.tar.zst` from a cached hash-verified `chatgpt 26.825.51511` package. The official packaged-manifest checker returned `correct: true`, mode 600, current host, and both extension origins. [Task 8]\n- If the original checkout is dirty/conflicted or `.git` is read-only, use `git ls-remote origin refs/heads/main`, create a clean detached clone/worktree at that SHA, and preserve ignored `linux-features/features.json` from the original so enabled features/settings are retained. The validated isolated build used `aac6178`, cached hash-verified `chatgpt 26.901.20858`, and preserved 29 enabled feature IDs. [Task 9]\n- Validate package identity/layout, launcher `--diagnose`, shell syntax, patched Electron `node --check`, upstream hash/provenance, and absence of upstream `/usr`/`/etc` payload paths. The resulting package was not installed; live ChatGPT Community/Thorium bridge proof remains pending. [Task 9]\n- At the observed state, `main` was `0ee586f`, ahead 1/behind 16 with `MERGE_HEAD=aac6178`, four unresolved files, staged/untracked work, and read-only `.git/FETCH_HEAD`. Preserve it; use a writable, network-enabled shell for fetch/pull. Public main was `23c55eb`, so cached `aac6178` cannot be claimed current. [Task 10]\n"},"sessionId":"01a0cc87-495d-7e22-87ae-bd213dd99181","timestamp":"2026-09-23T04:30:56.981Z","toolInput":{"command":"sed -n '2558,2575p' /home/chris/.codex/memories/MEMORY.md"},"toolName":"Bash","toolOutput":"- Compare a signed upstream ASAR and installed patched ASAR with `node --check` before changing features: the upstream `26.810.50856` ASAR parsed, while the installed patched ASAR failed at main bundle line 506. Isolation identified `computer-use-linux` and its `nix-staging-permissions` patch. [Task 4]\n- In `linux-features/computer-use-linux/patch.js`, a patch injected into a minified comma expression must stay an expression. Wrap cleanup as `await(async()=>{try{...}finally{...}})()` rather than inserting bare `try/finally`; retain parser regression coverage for the comma-expression fixture. Focused tests, both isolated and complete 16-feature `node --check`, and `git diff --check` passed. [Task 4]\n- The GUI updater rebuilds from frozen `/opt/codex-desktop/update-builder`; after fixing this checkout, an authorized recovery is `make install-native`, not Check for Updates. The fixed source was not installed/restarted in this rollout. [Task 4]\n- On CachyOS/Niri/Wayland, the bundled Computer Use backend is the correct path; doctor checks AT-SPI, Niri window control, portal input/screenshots, `/dev/uinput`, and development input. Backend/plugin registration is separate from UI visibility: use `CODEX_LINUX_ENABLE_COMPUTER_USE_UI=1` for a one-build UI opt-in. [Task 1]\n- `enabled-feature-drift` is a descriptor/config mismatch, not a bypass condition. The 27-feature attempt became accepted-with-warnings/zero blockers only after removing the eight drifted ids and rerunning direct `scripts/validate-upstream-dmg.js --enforce`; 19 features remained enabled. [Task 1]\n- After `make install-native`, compare running app/webview/plugin start times to package install time, inspect stale hashed-asset errors, fully terminate old processes, then restart. `Failed to fetch dynamically imported module` here came from an old Electron renderer after `/opt/codex-desktop` replacement, not missing Computer Use payload; post-restart cache/install SHA and doctor matched. [Task 1]\n- `linux-features/shared-app-server-socket/patch.js` must retain upstream `getConfigOverrides` and spawn the shared `app-server` authority with each generated value as repeated `-c <override>` pairs. A bundled-Codex check accepted `codex_app` as `transport: stdio` and completed `thread/start`; focused socket regression coverage passed. [Task 5]\n- Upstream `main` was fast-forwarded from `6bd3816` to `b95f2f5` while tracked local work was retained in `stash@{0}: On main: codex-pull-preserve-20260827`. Conflict intent was upstream’s current Computer Use descriptor architecture plus the local shared-socket override regression and upstream Unix-socket path helper. [Task 5]\n- Thorium has two independent surfaces: `scripts/extension-ids.json` controls discovery, while `scripts/installManifest.mjs` embeds native-messaging manifest directories. `patchManifestInstaller()` in `linux-features/thorium-chrome-plugin/patch-chrome-plugin.js` idempotently adds `.config/thorium/NativeMessagingHosts` after the Google Chrome-for-Testing anchor. [Task 6]\n- A stale Thorium manifest can yield `No compatible Codex app-server entry was found` even with registry support, because host identity is checked against current `extensionHostPath`. The observed legacy host was cache release `26.803.81509`; current runtime was `26.820.71523`. Tests passed: Thorium feature 6/6, framework 1/1, syntax checks, and `git diff --check`. [Task 6]\n- For signed package `26.820.71523`, stale feature patches fail critical drift checks: use current `origin/main` (`b95f2f5`) in a detached worktree and run `node --test scripts/lib/linux-features.test.js linux-features/*/test.js`. The validated native package had 31 user-facing features, 69 applied patches, 762 pass/0 fail, and `pacman -Qkk` found 0 altered files. `directory-only-working-tree-watch` conflicts with `shallow-repository-watches`; omit internal Nix-only `nix-store-bundled-marketplace-permissions` from native packages. [Task 7]\n- `launcher/start.sh.template` must export `CODEX_HOME`, `CODEX_LINUX_APP_DIR`, `CODEX_LINUX_APP_STATE_DIR`, `CODEX_LINUX_FEATURES_DIR`, and `CODEX_LINUX_LAUNCHER_LOG`; prelaunch/cold-start hooks receive app/state/cache-log paths, and after-exit additionally receives Electron exit status. Prove the contract with `launcher provides the documented runtime hook contract`, `node --test launcher/start.test.js` (8/8), and `bash tests/scripts_smoke.sh`. [Task 7]\n- Verify installed state through `/opt/codex-desktop/resources/codex-linux-build-info.json`, patch report, `/opt/codex-desktop/start.sh --diagnose`, and Computer Use doctor. Read Aloud can use native `spd-say`/`espeak-ng` when Kokoro assets are absent. [Task 7]\n- Current upstream splits Thorium inventory between `browserDiagnostics` and `browserExtensions`; register Thorium in both. The shared Chromium registry now lives in `.vite/build/src-*.js`, so the feature needs an `extracted-app:pre-webview` descriptor. Prelaunch/cold-start hooks regenerate the manifest from the current cache; a stale host path can fail even when discovery is correct. [Task 8]\n- On an isolated detached worktree at `origin/main` `241435e`, Thorium focused tests were 9/9, and `make pacman` produced `/tmp/codex-desktop-linux-repair-ready/dist/codex-desktop-2026.08.30.155556-1-x86_64.pkg.tar.zst` from a cached hash-verified `chatgpt 26.825.51511` package. The official packaged-manifest checker returned `correct: true`, mode 600, current host, and both extension origins. [Task 8]\n- If the original checkout is dirty/conflicted or `.git` is read-only, use `git ls-remote origin refs/heads/main`, create a clean detached clone/worktree at that SHA, and preserve ignored `linux-features/features.json` from the original so enabled features/settings are retained. The validated isolated build used `aac6178`, cached hash-verified `chatgpt 26.901.20858`, and preserved 29 enabled feature IDs. [Task 9]\n- Validate package identity/layout, launcher `--diagnose`, shell syntax, patched Electron `node --check`, upstream hash/provenance, and absence of upstream `/usr`/`/etc` payload paths. The resulting package was not installed; live ChatGPT Community/Thorium bridge proof remains pending. [Task 9]\n- At the observed state, `main` was `0ee586f`, ahead 1/behind 16 with `MERGE_HEAD=aac6178`, four unresolved files, staged/untracked work, and read-only `.git/FETCH_HEAD`. Preserve it; use a writable, network-enabled shell for fetch/pull. Public main was `23c55eb`, so cached `aac6178` cannot be claimed current. [Task 10]\n"},"obs_mudluyo5_0da515d3eaa9":{"hookType":"post_tool_use","id":"obs_mudluyo5_0da515d3eaa9","origin":{"capturedAt":"2026-09-23T04:30:56.990Z","channel":"tool","detail":"Bash"},"raw":{"tool_input":{"command":"sed -n '1,180p' linux-features/thorium-chrome-plugin/README.md; sed -n '1,180p' linux-features/thorium-chrome-plugin/feature.json; sed -n '1,180p' docs/linux-computer-use.md"},"tool_name":"Bash","tool_output":"# Thorium Chrome Plugin Support\n\nThis optional Linux feature extends the bundled Chrome plugin to recognize\nThorium as a Chromium-family browser.\n\nIt is disabled by default because Thorium is a narrower browser variant that the\ncore Linux port does not regularly test. Enable it by adding the feature id to\n`linux-features/features.json` before building or installing:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"enabled\": [\n    \"thorium-chrome-plugin\"\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nWhen enabled, the feature:\n\n- adds Thorium to the bundled native-messaging manifest installer\n- registers Thorium in the staged Chrome plugin's diagnostic and extension\n  inventories\n  (`scripts/extension-ids.json`), which is where upstream reads install\n  commands, process names, profile directories, and native-messaging manifest\n  locations from\n- registers Thorium in the Electron-side shared Chromium registry\n- refreshes Thorium's native-host manifest before launch and after first-run\n  plugin cache creation\n\nThorium inherits the Chrome extension identity, because it loads the same\nextension build. Upstream moved this contract into the registry in\n26.803.41515; before that the same coverage needed seven separate patches\nagainst Chrome-only constants in individual plugin scripts.\n\nRun the focused tests with:\n\n```bash\nnode --test linux-features/thorium-chrome-plugin/test.js\n```\n{\n  \"id\": \"thorium-chrome-plugin\",\n  \"title\": \"Thorium Chrome Plugin Support\",\n  \"description\": \"Adds disabled-by-default Thorium support to the bundled Chrome plugin on Linux.\",\n  \"defaultEnabled\": false,\n  \"entrypoints\": {\n    \"patchDescriptors\": \"./patch.js\",\n    \"stageHook\": \"./stage.sh\"\n  },\n  \"resources\": [\n    {\n      \"source\": \"sync-manifest.js\",\n      \"target\": \".codex-linux/features/thorium-chrome-plugin/sync-manifest.js\",\n      \"mode\": \"0644\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"runtimeHooks\": {\n    \"prelaunch\": {\n      \"source\": \"sync-manifest.sh\",\n      \"name\": \"thorium-native-host.sh\",\n      \"mode\": \"0755\"\n    },\n    \"coldStart\": {\n      \"source\": \"sync-manifest.sh\",\n      \"name\": \"thorium-native-host.sh\",\n      \"mode\": \"0755\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n# Linux Computer Use\n\nLinux Computer Use is an opt-in UI surface backed by a native Rust MCP backend,\n`codex-computer-use-linux`. The official Linux package is the baseline, and the\ncommunity integration is disabled until the `computer-use-linux` feature is\nexplicitly enabled. Enabling it stages the Linux backend/plugin and the\nfeature-owned UI descriptors; none of them are default core patches.\n\nIn Settings → Computer use, **Any App**\ncontrols native access independently of browser access. Use that row to install\nor enable native access on a fresh profile.\n\nThe backend's capabilities are listed below. The in-app API exposes a subset;\nsee [supported operations](../linux-features/computer-use-linux/README.md#supported-operations)\nfor its limitations.\n\nIt supports:\n\n- app listing and accessibility trees through AT-SPI\n- screenshots through GNOME Shell DBus, the Codex GNOME Shell extension, or XDG Desktop Portal\n- window listing and focusing on GNOME, KWin/Plasma 5 and 6, Hyprland, Niri,\n  COSMIC, i3, and generic X11/EWMH window managers; GNOME extension and X11\n  windows can also be moved and resized\n- keyboard, text, click, scroll, and drag input through `/dev/uinput`, XDG\n  RemoteDesktop portal, `xdotool` on X11, or `ydotool`\n- pointer-direction feedback for the built-in V2 pet after successful click,\n  scroll, and drag actions\n\nThe in-app adapter returns a compact accessibility projection and suppresses an\nunchanged compact projection on repeated reads. Use `disableDiffing: true` for a\nfresh compact tree, or `compact: false` for complete backend metadata.\n`maxNodes`/`maxDepth` bound accessibility traversal; screenshot calls accept\n`maxWidth`, `maxHeight`, `maxBytes`, `scale`, `format`, and `quality`. Screenshot\nmethods already emit the image and should not be emitted a second time.\n\n## Runtime Dependencies\n\nInstall `ydotool` 1.0.3 or newer when you need the fallback input path. The\nbackend probes the exact absolute move, wheel move, click, delayed key, and\nstdin typing command shapes it emits. Earlier or incompatible CLIs are rejected\neven if `ydotoold` and its socket are present.\n\n```bash\n# Debian / Ubuntu\nsudo apt install ydotool\nsudo apt install ydotoold   # on Ubuntu releases that split the daemon\n\n# Fedora\nsudo dnf install ydotool\n\n# Arch / Manjaro\nsudo pacman -S ydotool\n\n# openSUSE\nsudo zypper install ydotool\n```\n\nThe preferred coordinate input path opens `/dev/uinput` directly, but that\ndevice provides pointer input only. Keyboard readiness still requires an XDG\nRemoteDesktop portal with keyboard support, `xdotool` on X11, or a compatible\n`ydotool` daemon and socket. Portal pointer support also requires the\nRemoteDesktop pointer methods, a monitor-capable ScreenCast source, and the\nmatching advertised device types. ScreenCast v2 and newer must also advertise\nthe hidden cursor mode that the runtime requests; v1 uses that mode by default.\n\nFor `ydotool`, run a daemon and make sure your user can access the socket:\n\n```bash\nsudo systemctl enable --now ydotoold\nsudo usermod -a -G input \"$USER\"\n```\n\nThen log out and back in.\n\nOn X11, install `xdotool` for layout-correct XTEST keyboard/text input and\ncoordinate clicks, and `wmctrl` plus `xprop` for generic EWMH window listing,\nfocus, move, and resize. `xdotool` is preferred only with a nonempty `DISPLAY`;\nydotool is used when it cannot be launched. Once xdotool starts, a failure or\ntimeout is returned and input is never replayed through ydotool. Override\nkeyboard selection with `COMPUTER_USE_LINUX_FORCE_YDOTOOL_KEYBOARD=1` or\n`CODEX_COMPUTER_USE_FORCE_YDOTOOL_KEYBOARD=1`; the corresponding\n`*_FORCE_XDOTOOL_KEYBOARD=1` names force XTEST when available. Set\n`COMPUTER_USE_LINUX_FORCE_YDOTOOL_POINTER=1` or\n`CODEX_COMPUTER_USE_FORCE_YDOTOOL_POINTER=1` to skip native-X11 xdotool clicks.\n\nSome distros name the unit `ydotool.service` instead of `ydotoold.service`, and\nsome install `/usr/bin/ydotoold` without a service unit. If the system unit path\nis awkward, a user-session service that binds `%t/.ydotool_socket` is also\nvalid.\n\nPortal packages are needed when your desktop relies on XDG Desktop Portal input\nor screenshots:\n\n- KDE Plasma: `xdg-desktop-portal-kde`\n- sway/wlroots: `xdg-desktop-portal-wlr`\n- Hyprland: `xdg-desktop-portal-hyprland`\n- GNOME: usually available by default\n\n`doctor` evaluates pointer and keyboard portal capability independently. A\nkeyboard-only or pointer-only RemoteDesktop implementation remains useful when\nits supported modality is complete, but pointer-only support does not make\nkeyboard readiness green.\n\nNiri window listing and exact focus use the `niri` command and the active\nsession's `NIRI_SOCKET`. The Computer Use backend hydrates `NIRI_SOCKET` for GUI\nstarts, but the socket must still belong to the active Niri session and be\nreachable by the desktop user.\n\nThe former `x11-ewmh-computer-use` alternative has been retired. The retained\n`computer-use-linux` backend owns generic X11/EWMH support on both official\narchitectures, so the x86-only duplicate no longer belongs in package builds.\n\n## Verify Readiness\n\nAfter enabling `computer-use-linux`, rebuilding, and reinstalling ChatGPT\nCommunity, ask Codex:\n\n> Check whether Linux Computer Use is ready\n\nYou can also run the backend directly:\n\n```bash\n./codex-app/resources/plugins/openai-bundled/plugins/unified-computer-use/bin/codex-computer-use-linux doctor\n./codex-app/resources/plugins/openai-bundled/plugins/unified-computer-use/bin/codex-computer-use-linux setup\n./codex-app/resources/plugins/openai-bundled/plugins/unified-computer-use/bin/codex-computer-use-linux apps\n./codex-app/resources/plugins/openai-bundled/plugins/unified-computer-use/bin/codex-computer-use-linux windows\n./codex-app/resources/plugins/openai-bundled/plugins/unified-computer-use/bin/codex-computer-use-linux screenshot\n```\n\n## Enable The In-App UI\n\nUse the optional-feature wizard and enable `computer-use-linux`:\n\n```bash\nmake setup-\n[...truncated]"},"sessionId":"01a0cc87-495d-7e22-87ae-bd213dd99181","timestamp":"2026-09-23T04:30:56.990Z","toolInput":{"command":"sed -n '1,180p' linux-features/thorium-chrome-plugin/README.md; sed -n '1,180p' linux-features/thorium-chrome-plugin/feature.json; sed -n '1,180p' docs/linux-computer-use.md"},"toolName":"Bash","toolOutput":"# Thorium Chrome Plugin Support\n\nThis optional Linux feature extends the bundled Chrome plugin to recognize\nThorium as a Chromium-family browser.\n\nIt is disabled by default because Thorium is a narrower browser variant that the\ncore Linux port does not regularly test. Enable it by adding the feature id to\n`linux-features/features.json` before building or installing:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"enabled\": [\n    \"thorium-chrome-plugin\"\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nWhen enabled, the feature:\n\n- adds Thorium to the bundled native-messaging manifest installer\n- registers Thorium in the staged Chrome plugin's diagnostic and extension\n  inventories\n  (`scripts/extension-ids.json`), which is where upstream reads install\n  commands, process names, profile directories, and native-messaging manifest\n  locations from\n- registers Thorium in the Electron-side shared Chromium registry\n- refreshes Thorium's native-host manifest before launch and after first-run\n  plugin cache creation\n\nThorium inherits the Chrome extension identity, because it loads the same\nextension build. Upstream moved this contract into the registry in\n26.803.41515; before that the same coverage needed seven separate patches\nagainst Chrome-only constants in individual plugin scripts.\n\nRun the focused tests with:\n\n```bash\nnode --test linux-features/thorium-chrome-plugin/test.js\n```\n{\n  \"id\": \"thorium-chrome-plugin\",\n  \"title\": \"Thorium Chrome Plugin Support\",\n  \"description\": \"Adds disabled-by-default Thorium support to the bundled Chrome plugin on Linux.\",\n  \"defaultEnabled\": false,\n  \"entrypoints\": {\n    \"patchDescriptors\": \"./patch.js\",\n    \"stageHook\": \"./stage.sh\"\n  },\n  \"resources\": [\n    {\n      \"source\": \"sync-manifest.js\",\n      \"target\": \".codex-linux/features/thorium-chrome-plugin/sync-manifest.js\",\n      \"mode\": \"0644\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"runtimeHooks\": {\n    \"prelaunch\": {\n      \"source\": \"sync-manifest.sh\",\n      \"name\": \"thorium-native-host.sh\",\n      \"mode\": \"0755\"\n    },\n    \"coldStart\": {\n      \"source\": \"sync-manifest.sh\",\n      \"name\": \"thorium-native-host.sh\",\n      \"mode\": \"0755\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n# Linux Computer Use\n\nLinux Computer Use is an opt-in UI surface backed by a native Rust MCP backend,\n`codex-computer-use-linux`. The official Linux package is the baseline, and the\ncommunity integration is disabled until the `computer-use-linux` feature is\nexplicitly enabled. Enabling it stages the Linux backend/plugin and the\nfeature-owned UI descriptors; none of them are default core patches.\n\nIn Settings → Computer use, **Any App**\ncontrols native access independently of browser access. Use that row to install\nor enable native access on a fresh profile.\n\nThe backend's capabilities are listed below. The in-app API exposes a subset;\nsee [supported operations](../linux-features/computer-use-linux/README.md#supported-operations)\nfor its limitations.\n\nIt supports:\n\n- app listing and accessibility trees through AT-SPI\n- screenshots through GNOME Shell DBus, the Codex GNOME Shell extension, or XDG Desktop Portal\n- window listing and focusing on GNOME, KWin/Plasma 5 and 6, Hyprland, Niri,\n  COSMIC, i3, and generic X11/EWMH window managers; GNOME extension and X11\n  windows can also be moved and resized\n- keyboard, text, click, scroll, and drag input through `/dev/uinput`, XDG\n  RemoteDesktop portal, `xdotool` on X11, or `ydotool`\n- pointer-direction feedback for the built-in V2 pet after successful click,\n  scroll, and drag actions\n\nThe in-app adapter returns a compact accessibility projection and suppresses an\nunchanged compact projection on repeated reads. Use `disableDiffing: true` for a\nfresh compact tree, or `compact: false` for complete backend metadata.\n`maxNodes`/`maxDepth` bound accessibility traversal; screenshot calls accept\n`maxWidth`, `maxHeight`, `maxBytes`, `scale`, `format`, and `quality`. Screenshot\nmethods already emit the image and should not be emitted a second time.\n\n## Runtime Dependencies\n\nInstall `ydotool` 1.0.3 or newer when you need the fallback input path. The\nbackend probes the exact absolute move, wheel move, click, delayed key, and\nstdin typing command shapes it emits. Earlier or incompatible CLIs are rejected\neven if `ydotoold` and its socket are present.\n\n```bash\n# Debian / Ubuntu\nsudo apt install ydotool\nsudo apt install ydotoold   # on Ubuntu releases that split the daemon\n\n# Fedora\nsudo dnf install ydotool\n\n# Arch / Manjaro\nsudo pacman -S ydotool\n\n# openSUSE\nsudo zypper install ydotool\n```\n\nThe preferred coordinate input path opens `/dev/uinput` directly, but that\ndevice provides pointer input only. Keyboard readiness still requires an XDG\nRemoteDesktop portal with keyboard support, `xdotool` on X11, or a compatible\n`ydotool` daemon and socket. Portal pointer support also requires the\nRemoteDesktop pointer methods, a monitor-capable ScreenCast source, and the\nmatching advertised device types. ScreenCast v2 and newer must also advertise\nthe hidden cursor mode that the runtime requests; v1 uses that mode by default.\n\nFor `ydotool`, run a daemon and make sure your user can access the socket:\n\n```bash\nsudo systemctl enable --now ydotoold\nsudo usermod -a -G input \"$USER\"\n```\n\nThen log out and back in.\n\nOn X11, install `xdotool` for layout-correct XTEST keyboard/text input and\ncoordinate clicks, and `wmctrl` plus `xprop` for generic EWMH window listing,\nfocus, move, and resize. `xdotool` is preferred only with a nonempty `DISPLAY`;\nydotool is used when it cannot be launched. Once xdotool starts, a failure or\ntimeout is returned and input is never replayed through ydotool. Override\nkeyboard selection with `COMPUTER_USE_LINUX_FORCE_YDOTOOL_KEYBOARD=1` or\n`CODEX_COMPUTER_USE_FORCE_YDOTOOL_KEYBOARD=1`; the corresponding\n`*_FORCE_XDOTOOL_KEYBOARD=1` names force XTEST when available. Set\n`COMPUTER_USE_LINUX_FORCE_YDOTOOL_POINTER=1` or\n`CODEX_COMPUTER_USE_FORCE_YDOTOOL_POINTER=1` to skip native-X11 xdotool clicks.\n\nSome distros name the unit `ydotool.service` instead of `ydotoold.service`, and\nsome install `/usr/bin/ydotoold` without a service unit. If the system unit path\nis awkward, a user-session service that binds `%t/.ydotool_socket` is also\nvalid.\n\nPortal packages are needed when your desktop relies on XDG Desktop Portal input\nor screenshots:\n\n- KDE Plasma: `xdg-desktop-portal-kde`\n- sway/wlroots: `xdg-desktop-portal-wlr`\n- Hyprland: `xdg-desktop-portal-hyprland`\n- GNOME: usually available by default\n\n`doctor` evaluates pointer and keyboard portal capability independently. A\nkeyboard-only or pointer-only RemoteDesktop implementation remains useful when\nits supported modality is complete, but pointer-only support does not make\nkeyboard readiness green.\n\nNiri window listing and exact focus use the `niri` command and the active\nsession's `NIRI_SOCKET`. The Computer Use backend hydrates `NIRI_SOCKET` for GUI\nstarts, but the socket must still belong to the active Niri session and be\nreachable by the desktop user.\n\nThe former `x11-ewmh-computer-use` alternative has been retired. The retained\n`computer-use-linux` backend owns generic X11/EWMH support on both official\narchitectures, so the x86-only duplicate no longer belongs in package builds.\n\n## Verify Readiness\n\nAfter enabling `computer-use-linux`, rebuilding, and reinstalling ChatGPT\nCommunity, ask Codex:\n\n> Check whether Linux Computer Use is ready\n\nYou can also run the backend directly:\n\n```bash\n./codex-app/resources/plugins/openai-bundled/plugins/unified-computer-use/bin/codex-computer-use-linux doctor\n./codex-app/resources/plugins/openai-bundled/plugins/unified-computer-use/bin/codex-computer-use-linux setup\n./codex-app/resources/plugins/openai-bundled/plugins/unified-computer-use/bin/codex-computer-use-linux apps\n./codex-app/resources/plugins/openai-bundled/plugins/unified-computer-use/bin/codex-computer-use-linux windows\n./codex-app/resources/plugins/openai-bundled/plugins/unified-computer-use/bin/codex-computer-use-linux screenshot\n```\n\n## Enable The In-App UI\n\nUse the optional-feature wizard and enable `computer-use-linux`:\n\n```bash\nmake setup-\n[...truncated]"},"obs_mudluyoh_b3c59990bc97":{"hookType":"post_tool_use","id":"obs_mudluyoh_b3c59990bc97","origin":{"capturedAt":"2026-09-23T04:30:57.000Z","channel":"tool","detail":"Bash"},"raw":{"tool_input":{"command":"ps -eo pid,ppid,comm | rg -i 'thorium|chatgpt|codex|chrome|electron' | head -60; ls -ld /run/user/$(id -u) /run/user/$(id -u)/bus 2>&1; env | rg '^(DISPLAY|WAYLAND_DISPLAY|XDG_RUNTIME_DIR|DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS)='"},"tool_name":"Bash","tool_output":"      1       0 codex\ndrwx------ 27 chris chris 980 Sep 23 00:16 /run/user/1000\nsrw-rw-rw-  1 chris chris   0 Sep 21 06:17 /run/user/1000/bus\nWAYLAND_DISPLAY=wayland-1\nDISPLAY=:0\nXDG_RUNTIME_DIR=/run/user/1000\nDBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS=unix:path=/run/user/1000/bus\n"},"sessionId":"01a0cc87-495d-7e22-87ae-bd213dd99181","timestamp":"2026-09-23T04:30:57.000Z","toolInput":{"command":"ps -eo pid,ppid,comm | rg -i 'thorium|chatgpt|codex|chrome|electron' | head -60; ls -ld /run/user/$(id -u) /run/user/$(id -u)/bus 2>&1; env | rg '^(DISPLAY|WAYLAND_DISPLAY|XDG_RUNTIME_DIR|DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS)='"},"toolName":"Bash","toolOutput":"      1       0 codex\ndrwx------ 27 chris chris 980 Sep 23 00:16 /run/user/1000\nsrw-rw-rw-  1 chris chris   0 Sep 21 06:17 /run/user/1000/bus\nWAYLAND_DISPLAY=wayland-1\nDISPLAY=:0\nXDG_RUNTIME_DIR=/run/user/1000\nDBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS=unix:path=/run/user/1000/bus\n"},"obs_mudluyoj_b7997a927a1c":{"hookType":"post_tool_use","id":"obs_mudluyoj_b7997a927a1c","origin":{"capturedAt":"2026-09-23T04:30:57.003Z","channel":"tool","detail":"Bash"},"raw":{"tool_input":{"command":"ls -ld /opt/codex-desktop /opt/codex-desktop/resources /home/chris/.config/thorium /home/chris/.config/thorium/NativeMessagingHosts 2>&1; rg --files /home/chris/.config/thorium/NativeMessagingHosts /home/chris/.config/google-chrome/NativeMessagingHosts /home/chris/.config/chromium/NativeMessagingHosts 2>/dev/null | rg -i 'codex|openai|chatgpt' | head -30; ls -l /opt/codex-desktop/resources/codex-linux-build-info.json /opt/codex-desktop/resources/codex-linux-patch-report.json 2>&1"},"tool_name":"Bash","tool_output":"drwx------ 1 chris  chris  1278 Sep 23 00:25 /home/chris/.config/thorium\ndrwx------ 1 chris  chris    60 Sep  6 13:00 /home/chris/.config/thorium/NativeMessagingHosts\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 nobody nobody  868 Sep 22 21:35 /opt/codex-desktop\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 nobody nobody  556 Sep 22 21:35 /opt/codex-desktop/resources\n/home/chris/.config/chromium/NativeMessagingHosts/com.openai.codexextension.json\n/home/chris/.config/thorium/NativeMessagingHosts/com.openai.codexextension.json\nls: cannot access '/opt/codex-desktop/resources/codex-linux-patch-report.json': No such file or directory\n-rw-r--r-- 1 nobody nobody 2539 Sep 22 21:34 /opt/codex-desktop/resources/codex-linux-build-info.json\n"},"sessionId":"01a0cc87-495d-7e22-87ae-bd213dd99181","timestamp":"2026-09-23T04:30:57.003Z","toolInput":{"command":"ls -ld /opt/codex-desktop /opt/codex-desktop/resources /home/chris/.config/thorium /home/chris/.config/thorium/NativeMessagingHosts 2>&1; rg --files /home/chris/.config/thorium/NativeMessagingHosts /home/chris/.config/google-chrome/NativeMessagingHosts /home/chris/.config/chromium/NativeMessagingHosts 2>/dev/null | rg -i 'codex|openai|chatgpt' | head -30; ls -l /opt/codex-desktop/resources/codex-linux-build-info.json /opt/codex-desktop/resources/codex-linux-patch-report.json 2>&1"},"toolName":"Bash","toolOutput":"drwx------ 1 chris  chris  1278 Sep 23 00:25 /home/chris/.config/thorium\ndrwx------ 1 chris  chris    60 Sep  6 13:00 /home/chris/.config/thorium/NativeMessagingHosts\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 nobody nobody  868 Sep 22 21:35 /opt/codex-desktop\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 nobody nobody  556 Sep 22 21:35 /opt/codex-desktop/resources\n/home/chris/.config/chromium/NativeMessagingHosts/com.openai.codexextension.json\n/home/chris/.config/thorium/NativeMessagingHosts/com.openai.codexextension.json\nls: cannot access '/opt/codex-desktop/resources/codex-linux-patch-report.json': No such file or directory\n-rw-r--r-- 1 nobody nobody 2539 Sep 22 21:34 /opt/codex-desktop/resources/codex-linux-build-info.json\n"},"obs_mudlv9z3_e6dd94e7f908":{"hookType":"post_tool_use","id":"obs_mudlv9z3_e6dd94e7f908","origin":{"capturedAt":"2026-09-23T04:31:1